  "title": "OntoAR-HAR and CHEAR: A Comparative Framework for Human Activity Recognition",
  "summary": "OntoAR-HAR provides an ontology-guided agentic RAG framework that decouples sensor-based motion data from static population knowledge, while CHEAR introduces confusion-hierarchy evidence-aware reasoning for cost-efficient human activity recognition. Both frameworks address challenges in multi-modal reasoning and decision-making.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "OntoAR-HAR integrates static population knowledge with personalized memory for LLM inference.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "CHEAR organizes candidates and discriminative knowledge around observed activity confusions.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "Both methods achieve state-of-the-art performance in human activity recognition across datasets.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
